faq:
  - q: "What are resume keywords?"
    a: "Resume keywords are the specific skills, tools, technologies, titles, and qualifications that ATS systems and recruiters search for when evaluating candidates. They include: exact role titles (Senior Software Engineer, not just Software Engineer), technologies and tools (Python, Kubernetes, Salesforce), methodologies (Agile, STAR, Six Sigma), certifications (CPA, CFA, PMP, AWS Certified), and soft-skill phrases used in job descriptions (cross-functional leadership, stakeholder management)."
  - q: "How do I find the right keywords for my resume?"
    a: "Extract them from the job description. Paste 3–5 target job descriptions into a document. Highlight every skill, tool, technology, methodology, and qualification that appears repeatedly. The terms that appear in 3+ postings are your priority keywords."
  - q: "Where should keywords appear on my resume?"
    a: "Four places: (1) Professional summary — 3–5 of the highest-priority terms. (2) Skills section — explicit list of all relevant technical terms, tools, and methodologies. (3) Experience bullets — in context, describing actual usage. (4) Education or Certifications — degree, certification, and institution names."
  - q: "How many keywords should my resume have?"
    a: "There is no exact number, but a resume optimized for a specific role should include 15–25 targeted keywords. The Skills section alone should have 12–20 terms. Do not stuff keywords artificially — every keyword should appear in a legitimate context."
  - q: "Do I need different keywords for different jobs?"
    a: "Yes — this is exactly why tailoring matters. Each job description uses slightly different language. 'Machine learning' and 'ML' may be treated as separate terms. 'Data analysis' and 'data analytics' may not match. Mirror the exact phrasing from each posting for the highest ATS match rate."
